16. MULTI-AGENT VS MONOLITHIC AGENT
======================================================================

MONOLITHIC AGENT

Example Initial Development Cost:
$50K

Characteristics:
- Lower initial cost
- Simpler starting point
- Limited complexity handling
- Maintenance burden grows with complexity
- New changes can affect the entire system
- Larger failure impact


----------------------------------------------------------------------

MULTI-AGENT SYSTEM

Example Initial Development Cost:
$150K

Characteristics:
- Higher initial investment
- Strong complexity handling
- Modular components
- Easier specialization
- New capabilities can be implemented through additional agents
- Failures can potentially be isolated to individual components


======================================================================
17. TECHNICAL VS BUSINESS COMMUNICATION
======================================================================

TECHNICAL VERSION:

"A monolithic agent has tight coupling, which creates maintenance
burden.

A multi-agent system uses loose coupling and modular design patterns."


BUSINESS VERSION:

"Think of it as one person versus a team.

If one person handles everything, that person eventually becomes a
bottleneck.

A team distributes responsibilities among specialists.

The team costs more initially, but it can scale responsibilities as
requirements grow without repeatedly rebuilding the entire solution."


Key Principle:

Don't remove the technical explanation.

Translate its consequences into business language.


